# Every treble note read from the two landmarks, middle C on its short line and treble G on the
# second line: the lines E G B D F, the spaces F A C E, one letter a step.
lesson music-reading-the-treble-staff v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Reading the treble staff"
  goal "Name any note on the treble staff from the landmarks and play it."

  look {
    scene 28x17 {
      grandstaff gs notes=["E4", "G4", "B4", "D5", "F5"] letters=true lit=[] landmarks=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      The treble staff has five lines, and the clef curls round the second one: that line is G.
      Every line and every space is one letter, so from G the lines climb G, B, D, F and the
      spaces between them are A, C, E. Below G the bottom line is E, and below that, on its own
      short line, is middle C. Name a note from the nearest landmark and count the steps.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.bottom-line
    level easy {
      worked music.treble-name i=1 level=medium
    }
    practice music.treble-name count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.play-treble
    practice music.which-line count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice music.line-or-space count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.name-near-landmark count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=2 hard-level=medium
    level hard {
      show music.treble-name i=8 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show music.tune-count k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.two-from-landmark k=1 r=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.play-four
  }
  remember {
    say "Treble G is on the second line. Lines E G B D F, spaces F A C E, one letter a step."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a treble note is named by counting steps from the nearest landmark, middle
    C on its short line or treble G on the second line, one letter for every line and every
    space. The look is the five line notes with their letters and the way in is the bottom line.
    The core names ringed notes across the staff, plays a note read from it (the machine hears
    the key), says which line a letter sits on, whether a note is on a line or in a space, and
    names a note near a landmark. The wrong idea to watch for is counting lines only, so that
    two lines up is read as two steps; ask the child to say every letter, line and space, out
    loud. The story counts the space notes in a bandstand tune, the two-star question names a
    note two steps from a landmark with the reason, and the three-star question is a four-note
    tune read and played, proved by the pitches. If the naming is slow, stay another day with
    the landmarks alone, then the notes one step from them.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level F in the space above the bottom line is worked once in the teacher's pen before the notes nearest the landmarks, E, F and A, the lines asked are the bottom and the second, the line or space is asked for E, F or G, the note near a landmark is a step away, the story's tune stays in the bottom spaces, and the two-star is two steps above G."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes to name are the high ones, C to G above the staff's middle, with a second, the top G, in the core, the lines asked are the middle, fourth and top, the line or space is asked for B to E, the note near a landmark is a skip away, the story's tune climbs to E in the top space, and the two-star is two steps above middle C."
  }
}
